  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Widespread smoke continues to degrade air quality, with the\n   highest impacts across northwest Montana. \n\n-  Elevated to near-critical fire weather across Southwest Montana\n   today. \n\n-  A gradual cooling trend by the middle of next week; rain and \n   thunderstorm chances, primarily along the Continental Divide. \n\nAs with the last several days, the Northern Rockies will remain\nunder a dry westerly flow aloft through Tuesday. A large ridge of\nhigh pressure remains centered to the south, with the Northern\nRockies situated on the northern periphery of the ridge. Fire\nweather remains a concern, with hot, dry, and breezy conditions\nbringing areas of near-critical to localized critical fire\nweather, focused along and east of the divide in Southwest\nMontana. Areas in the vicinity of Dillon have the highest \nprobability (40-50%) of reaching Red Flag Warning criteria as \ngusts reach 20-30 mph by mid-late afternoon. \n\nWildfire smoke continues to degrade air quality and visibility\nacross the region. Unhealthy to very unhealthily Air Quality\nIndexes have been observed along and north of the I-90 corridor in\nnorthwest Montana. These impacts are driven by significant\nwildfire activity upstream in Oregon and Washington. Looking ahead\nto this afternoon, deeper mixing associated with an upper level\ndisturbance will help to provide improved ventilation and perhaps\nimprovements to visibility. This will largely depend on how active\nwildfires become upstream. \n\nModel guidance continues to highlight a cooling trend by Wednesday\ninto Thursday as a trough of low pressure moves southeastward from\nBritish Columbia. Shower and thunderstorm activity will initially\nfocus along the USA-Canadian border and divide in northwest\nMontana Wednesday afternoon, before spreading southward across\nmuch of the area by Thursday. This change will be driven by\nincreased atmospheric moisture and instability as deepening\nsouth-southwest flow moves monsoon moisture into the region. \nFurthermore, model guidance suggests a cold front will track \nsouthward along the divide Wednesday night into Thursday morning, \nbringing a north-northeast wind shift across western Montana. \n\nBy Friday and into next weekend, the focus for thunderstorm\nactivity will shift south into Southwest Montana as the region\ntransitions into a southwesterly flow pattern.",
